--TEST--
a deployment granted `net.listen` may bind and still may not dial, and the refusal names the grant it is missing — `rule:security/net-listen-is-a-separate-grant-from-net-connect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Which capability an opening asks is decided by what the program is *doing*,
// not by the transport it does it over — so the bind below succeeds under a
// grant that buys the dial after it nothing at all. A `net.listen` that widened
// `net.connect` would make every program that serves a port a program that may
// reach anywhere, which is the direction this split exists to close.
var $door = Core\Net::listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
echo "bound: ", ($door->port() > 0) as string, "\n";

// Its own port, and still refused: the grant is asked of the host and this
// deployment granted no host. That the address is one the program could
// plainly already reach is the point — authority here is not derived from
// reachability.
try {
    Core\Net::connect("127.0.0.1", $door->port(), 1s);
    echo "reached it", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

$door->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
# One key. `net.connect` is absent, which is `rule:security/capability-roster-is-closed`'s
# deny-by-default: a grant that is not written is not held.
[capabilities.net]
listen = true
--EXPECT--
bound: 1
Core\Net::connect needs the capability `net.connect` for 127.0.0.1, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.net]`
